Hello Xenomai users and developers,

for my grad. assignment I am comparing Xenomai to some other co-kernel
implementations (all using the Linux kernel).
The documentation of this project has been great, and the archives and
source code has been a great help in finding my way around the system.

However, I've hit a little bump while testing some scheduling latency using
the POSIX skin.
The latencies I'm registering are okay, but they do differ when picking
different periods.
I have excluded my program from causing this issue, as the same thing
happens when using the latency tool supplied by Xenomai.

I have run some traces to determine where the problem might be (see
attachments), with one run using a 1 ms period and another using a 10 ms
period.
I've noticed a big difference between __ipipe_syscall_root on offset 0x108
and in xnpod_resume_thread.
The latencies get even better when using a periodicity of 100 microseconds,
and worse still at 100 milliseconds.

Of course, a latency difference of 10 microseconds isn't that bad, but I'm
wondering why there is a difference at all?
I can attach my program as well if needed, but it simply loops in a thread
and using clock_nanosleep with CLOCK_MONOTONIC, and pushing the results to a
display thread with a message queue.
Still, the same occurs when using the supplied latency tool.

The system I'm testing this on is a MPC8548E , with Linux-2.6.29-DENX and
Xenomai 2.5.0 .

Thanks in advance for your help.

Gerald Otter

p.s. I've read through the mailing lists and the TROUBLESHOOTING files, but
could not find any references to a similar issue.

